1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,292
README L M: can a README run a language model?

2
00:00:03,292 --> 00:00:09,263
This is a short, honest tour of three answers, what actually runs in each, and where the limits

3
00:00:09,263 --> 00:00:09,515
are.

4
00:00:10,510 --> 00:00:12,519
A README is a static document.

5
00:00:12,519 --> 00:00:18,880
GitHub strips scripts and styles, and even its math rendering only typesets, it cannot define a

6
00:00:18,880 --> 00:00:19,281
macro.

7
00:00:19,281 --> 00:00:20,420
We measured that.

8
00:00:20,420 --> 00:00:26,111
But an S V G image does render, filters included, and GitHub renders MediaWiki pages.

9
00:00:26,111 --> 00:00:29,392
So the real question is: who does the arithmetic?

10
00:00:30,390 --> 00:00:32,020
Version A is the real thing.

11
00:00:32,020 --> 00:00:37,494
A real GPT-2 small and a six hundred thousand parameter question answering model are stored as

12
00:00:37,494 --> 00:00:42,443
Markdown, and a small engine, also written in Markdown, runs them on a GitHub Action.

13
00:00:42,443 --> 00:00:47,800
Version B is a tiny network written as MediaWiki formulas, so GitHub's own renderer does the

14
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:51,410
arithmetic, and 40 of 40 live predictions match the reference.

15
00:00:51,410 --> 00:00:55,894
Version C is a tiny network drawn as S V G filters, computed by your browser.

16
00:00:55,894 --> 00:00:58,689
B and C are toys, and they are not transformers.

17
00:00:59,690 --> 00:01:01,589
In version A, you open an issue.

18
00:01:01,589 --> 00:01:06,991
A workflow extracts the engine from a Markdown document and runs the forward pass in numpy.

19
00:01:06,991 --> 00:01:12,273
The reply is posted on the issue, and the profile animation is rewritten with the answer.

20
00:01:13,270 --> 00:01:18,617
GPT-2 small does not fit in one README, so its weights are split across 339 Markdown shards.

21
00:01:18,617 --> 00:01:23,964
The question answering model is small enough that its weights fit inside the profile README.

22
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:27,631
A different route is precomputed link graphs.

23
00:01:27,631 --> 00:01:31,073
For 12 real GPT-2 prompts we compiled click through pages.

24
00:01:31,073 --> 00:01:36,356
This chart shows how long a sampled continuation stays inside a two thousand state graph.

25
00:01:36,356 --> 00:01:40,689
Confident prompts compile well, open ended text leaves the graph quickly.

26
00:01:40,689 --> 00:01:44,428
This is navigation of precomputed predictions, not computation.

27
00:01:45,430 --> 00:01:51,519
For the question answering model, 701 questions compile into 72,061 raw states.

28
00:01:51,519 --> 00:01:58,918
Merging runs with a single confident continuation shrinks that to 1,425, about 51 times smaller.

29
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:05,230
On the SVG side, every individual operation was built as a filter graph and checked bit exact

30
00:02:05,230 --> 00:02:07,172
in headless Chrome: 26 of 26 pass.

31
00:02:07,172 --> 00:02:08,828
The blocker is chaining them.

32
00:02:08,828 --> 00:02:14,195
A full transformer token step does not render in Chrome, which is why the shipped network is a

33
00:02:14,195 --> 00:02:15,109
two layer M L P.

34
00:02:16,110 --> 00:02:17,131
The limits matter.

35
00:02:17,131 --> 00:02:20,932
The fast version computes on a GitHub runner, outside the renderer.

36
00:02:20,932 --> 00:02:24,393
The pure markup versions compute inside it, but only as toys.

37
00:02:24,393 --> 00:02:28,081
No substrate we tried runs a GPT two sized model inside a README.

38
00:02:28,081 --> 00:02:33,471
The question answering model is mostly memorisation, and GPT two is a base model that continues

39
00:02:33,471 --> 00:02:35,003
text, not a chat assistant.

40
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:41,113
The code, the probe reports for each substrate, and the full original README are all in the

41
00:02:41,113 --> 00:02:41,394
repo.

